/-!
# Vélu quotient isogeny via places, odd order case

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.exists_veluFunctionFieldHom_restrictAlong_placeOfPoint_eq`
(`acd8e718-18d4-56b2-b4fe-299ba6bb61cd`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `200e3821-d296-5b22-a15a-da3c41ece41b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over
$F$ which is elliptic, let $Q$ be a point of its affine model and $n$ a natural number with
$\mathrm{addOrderOf}\,Q = 2n+1$. Write $S =$ `W.oddOrderSummingSet Q n`, the finite set of
coordinate pairs of $Q, 2Q, \dots, nQ$, and let $W' =$ `W.veluQuotient S` be the Weierstrass curve
with the same $a_1,a_2,a_3$ and with $a_4$ replaced by $a_4 - 5\,\mathrm{veluTSum}(S)$ and $a_6$ by
$a_6 - b_2\,\mathrm{veluTSum}(S) - 7\,\mathrm{veluWSum}(S)$. Assume $\Delta(W') \ne 0$, that $W'$ is
elliptic, and that both affine models carry a genus-one place gate (a bijection between points and
places of the function field, all places of degree $1$), its centredness hypothesis, and the
Abel-theorem hypothesis (a degree-zero divisor is principal exactly when its point sum vanishes).
Then there exists an $F$-algebra map $\iota : F(W') \to F(W)$ which is integral and along which
$F(W)$ is a finite $F(W')$-module, such that: the degree $[F(W):F(W')]$ along $\iota$ equals $2n+1$;
for every witness of the pushforward norm formula along $\iota$, the resulting additive map $W(F)
\to W'(F)$ on points has kernel the subgroup of integer multiples of $Q$; every $P$ in that subgroup
has the place $\mathrm{placeOfPoint}(P)$ restricting along $\iota$ to the place of the origin of
$W'$; and for every nonsingular affine point $(x,y)$ of $W$ not in that subgroup, the pair
$(\mathrm{veluX}_S(x), \mathrm{veluY}_S(x,y))$ is a nonsingular point of $W'$ and the place of
$(x,y)$ restricts along $\iota$ to the place of that point.

This is the function-field form of Vélu's construction of the quotient isogeny by a cyclic subgroup
of odd order $2n+1$: the inclusion of function fields, its degree, the kernel of the induced map on
points, and the pointwise identification of that map with Vélu's explicit formulae, all expressed
through restriction of places. It is the source of the separate statements isolating the kernel as
the subgroup generated by $Q$, and of the comparison of $j$-invariants of a curve and of its odd
Vélu quotient.

